From f6ec7eacdaea236d9b8e5ae336790a7eb0414a51 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Braden <snufflyy@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 10:18:21 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] ppu_guest_call: a callback stack per host thread and nesting
 depth

ppu_guest_call and ppu_guest_call_ct ran every guest callback with r1 at
the fixed address 0xCFFE0000, on every host thread and at every nesting
depth. Two callbacks at once on different host threads (a GCM handler on
an interrupt thread while a guest thread pumps it from an HLE call), or a
callback whose HLE call delivers another callback, built their frames on
the same bytes. 0xCFFE0000 is also inside RSX local memory (0xC0000000,
localSize 256 MB).

Each (host thread, depth) pair now gets its own stack (PPU_CB_STACK_SIZE,
default 256 KB, guard page at the bottom), carved from the top of the PPU
stack region by moving g_vm_stack_alloc.region_end down while thread
stacks keep growing up from VM_STACK_BASE. A host thread that ends gives
its stacks back to a pool. Both entry points share one body.

tools/test_ppu_guest_call_stacks.py builds the real ppu_loader.cpp with
two threads inside a callback at once, a nested callback, placement and
recycling checks. On the old code 6 checks fail (both threads' frames at
0xCFFDFC00, outer and inner frames the same); now all pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
